# Engineering Economics
#
# A battery of worked problems on the time value of money, present worth, rate of return, depreciation, and capital budgeting, each with a check a reader can run by hand.

#>engineering_economics
#:layer #!pragmatics
#:domain #!engineering
#:form #!probe_battery
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!sourced
#:valence #!boon

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Engineering Economics"
 ~#claim: "A cash flow is worth less the later it arrives, so engineers compare alternatives by moving every flow to one moment at a stated interest rate."
 ~#proof: "Fisher set out interest as the price of time, Grant and Newnan give the standard comparisons, and Hirshleifer shows where the internal rate of return misleads."
 ~#door: "Work the capital recovery problem by hand, then check it by discounting the payments back."
 ~#audience: "engineering students and anyone weighing a purchase that pays back over years"
 continuity: "Engineering Economics | worth less"
 cold_open: "Money that arrives later is worth less, so every engineering choice is weighed at one moment and one rate."
}

